1
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:59,118
Full of Surprises

2
00:01:59,118 --> 00:02:04,096
The mailman just dropped some mail in our box, Grandpa.

3
00:02:04,096 --> 00:02:08,052
Probably a lot of advertising and bills.

4
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:09,412
Why don't you write to me, Robbie,

5
00:02:09,412 --> 00:02:31,876
so I can get some interesting mail?

6
00:02:31,876 --> 00:02:34,854
You were right, Grandpa.

7
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:39,032
Advertising, bills, bills, advertising.

8
00:02:39,032 --> 00:02:42,477
Yes. It's just like I said, Robbie.

9
00:02:42,477 --> 00:02:44,568
Nothing interesting.

10
00:02:44,568 --> 00:02:46,766
You won't believe it, Grandpa,

11
00:02:46,766 --> 00:02:50,017
but there's a letter here addressed to you,

12
00:02:50,017 --> 00:02:53,051
Mr. Malcolm Stewart, and it looks like a personal letter.

13
00:02:53,051 --> 00:02:55,024
Oh, it must be a bill.

14
00:02:55,024 --> 00:02:56,500
I don't think so.

15
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,760
The return name and address is

16
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:00,678
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

17
00:03:00,678 --> 00:03:04,634
You're joking. Pete Waters?

18
00:03:04,634 --> 00:03:07,612
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

19
00:03:07,612 --> 00:03:09,724
You know him?

20
00:03:09,724 --> 00:03:11,442
Do I know Pete Waters?

21
00:03:11,442 --> 00:03:13,567
You bet I do!

22
00:03:13,567 --> 00:03:19,523
He was my roommate in college.

23
00:03:19,523 --> 00:03:24,168
He visited with Grandma and me in Florida

24
00:03:24,168 --> 00:03:31,435
about five years ago.

25
00:03:31,435 --> 00:03:33,812
What does he say?

26
00:03:33,812 --> 00:03:34,413
Is he OK?

27
00:03:34,413 --> 00:03:35,613
Yeah, he's fine.

28
00:03:35,613 --> 00:03:37,864
Just fine.

29
00:03:37,864 --> 00:03:40,369
He's writing to invite me

30
00:03:40,369 --> 00:03:43,347
to spend a weekend with him at his farm.

31
00:03:43,347 --> 00:03:45,490
He's planning a get-together

32
00:03:45,490 --> 00:03:49,005
with two or three other college friends.

33
00:03:49,005 --> 00:03:53,691
A kind of a fifty-year anniversary reunion.

34
00:03:53,691 --> 00:03:55,259
Sounds like fun.

35
00:03:55,259 --> 00:03:58,237
Fifty years? Wow!

36
00:03:58,237 --> 00:04:01,215
It sounds like fun to me, too, Robbie.

37
00:04:01,215 --> 00:04:04,072
What kind of farm does he have?

38
00:04:04,072 --> 00:04:05,153
I've never been there, Robbie,

39
00:04:05,153 --> 00:04:09,931
but he has chickens and cows and all.

40
00:04:09,931 --> 00:04:11,816
That means fresh eggs and fresh milk.

41
00:04:11,816 --> 00:04:14,754
Does he have a family?

42
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:16,105
No, he doesn't, Robbie.

43
00:04:16,105 --> 00:04:18,105
He never married.

44
00:04:18,105 --> 00:04:20,088
He's not as lucky as I am

45
00:04:20,088 --> 00:04:23,540
to have a family and grandchildren.

46
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,238
I'm a lucky man.

47
00:04:26,238 --> 00:04:28,016
How come he never got married?

48
00:04:28,016 --> 00:04:30,994
That's a good question, Robbie.

49
00:04:30,994 --> 00:04:32,994
A very good question.

50
00:04:32,994 --> 00:04:34,303
He never married because

51
00:04:34,303 --> 00:04:38,430
the girl he was in love with in college married someone else.

52
00:04:38,430 --> 00:04:40,928
As simple as that.

53
00:04:40,928 --> 00:04:42,906
He never got over it.

54
00:04:42,906 --> 00:04:44,906
He must have loved her very much.

55
00:04:44,906 --> 00:04:47,684
Yes. Very much.

56
00:04:47,684 --> 00:04:48,862
Lillian Winters.

57
00:04:48,862 --> 00:04:51,840
She was in our class.

58
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:53,400
And what happened?

59
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:54,818
She was in love with Donald McGrath,

60
00:04:54,818 --> 00:04:57,796
the quarterback on our football team.

61
00:04:57,796 --> 00:05:00,774
Football players are always popular with the ladies.

62
00:05:00,774 --> 00:05:06,112
She liked Pete, and they went to dances together.

63
00:05:06,112 --> 00:05:08,890
But her heart was with Donald.

64
00:05:08,890 --> 00:05:10,208
Did he ever get over it?

65
00:05:10,208 --> 00:05:12,041
No, he never did.

66
00:05:12,041 --> 00:05:13,353
Where is she today?

67
00:05:13,353 --> 00:05:15,353
I don't know.

68
00:05:15,353 --> 00:05:18,235
Maybe Lillian will be at the reunion.

69
00:05:18,235 --> 00:05:19,975
You think so?

70
00:05:19,975 --> 00:05:21,620
Pete's full of surprises.

71
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:24,598
I wish I could go there with you, Grandpa.

72
00:05:24,598 --> 00:05:27,576
What do you think the surprise will be?

73
00:05:27,576 --> 00:05:30,554
With Pete, you never know, Robbie.

74
00:05:30,554 --> 00:05:35,892
Won't it be exciting to see all your college friends there again?

75
00:05:35,892 --> 00:05:36,509
It is already.

76
00:05:36,509 --> 00:05:42,465
I'm kind of excited about going now.

77
00:05:42,465 --> 00:05:44,925
Next weekend ...

78
00:05:44,925 --> 00:05:47,586
sleep over Friday and Saturday night

79
00:05:47,586 --> 00:05:49,911
and come back Sunday.

80
00:05:49,911 --> 00:05:54,377
I can't wait!

81
00:05:54,377 --> 00:05:56,125
Don't you think you ought to call Pete

82
00:05:56,125 --> 00:05:57,355
and tell him you're coming?

83
00:05:57,355 --> 00:06:00,355
You're reading my mind, Robbie.
